  "response": "Disputes of this kind are adjudicated less by weighing doctrine against circumstance in the abstract than by asking which account generates expectations that the surviving record can confirm or disappoint. The historiography itself supplies the contrast. Edward Gibbon enumerated five secondary causes for the growth of Christianity; well over a century later, Rodney Stark published The Rise of Christianity in 1996. The methodological distance between the two is instructive. The Rise of Christianity received the Pulitzer Prize for History.\n\nStark argued that conversion to Christianity spread primarily along pre-existing social networks of kin, friends and neighbours, and that mode of diffusion produced a community growing, on his estimate, at about 40 percent per decade; in other words, on his reckoning the number of Christians increased by roughly two-fifths every ten years. A figure of that order demands no mass persuasion, only ordinary sociability compounded over generations. The content of the preaching, by contrast, did substantial work of its own: the movement's doctrine promised a personal salvation unavailable in the surrounding cults. The political order in which the movement arose was undergoing a crisis of legitimacy, and any adequate account must grant explanatory weight to at least one of the two, the doctrinal promise or the political crisis, and perhaps to both.\n\nThat a promise of personal salvation could recruit adherents on its own terms is evidence for the classic sociological thesis of ideational autonomy. Because Max Weber argued in The Protestant Ethic and the Spirit of Capitalism that religious ideas exert an independent causal influence on social behaviour, comparative analysis of movements holding similar doctrines in different political settings came to be used to isolate the causal weight of circumstance. Weber maintained as well that material conditions alone determine the content of religious doctrine, a position that rules out the independent efficacy he defended elsewhere; the two cannot both be true. The comparative procedure is in any case only as good as the sources fed into it, which is why the quarrel migrates from theory to documentation.\n\nWhat finally settles the question is not theoretical preference but material contemporaneous with the events, produced by hands that had no stake in the tradition's later self-understanding. Hagarism urged the use of contemporary non-Islamic sources for reconstructing the earliest phase of Islamic history, and that demand resulted in historians' employing coins, inscriptions and papyri as independent checks on later narrative accounts of religious change. Onomastic evidence, consisting of shifts in personal naming patterns, is used to estimate local rates of conversion. Each of these instruments converts a question about motives, which the sources rarely answer, into a question about distributions in time and space, which they sometimes do. A doctrinal explanation predicts growth wherever the message is intelligible, irrespective of regime; a circumstantial one predicts concentration where authority is weakest. When the datable, non-narrative record discriminates between those patterns, the dispute is no longer a contest of interpretive temperaments.",
